    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "definitions",
        "bindings",
        "scopes"
      ],
      "reason": "A name bound inside a function body -- by `const`/`let`/`var`, by a `for` or `catch` binding, or by destructuring anywhere -- is not declared. Only a module-scope, exported or ambient declarator whose name is a plain identifier is. Parameters are carried as the declaration's parameter shape rather than declared on their own, and the one exception is a parameter property (`constructor(private x: T)`), which really does declare a field of the class."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "definitions"
      ],
      "reason": "A function assigned to a name (`export const load = async () => {}`) is declared as a variable, not as a function. The grammar gives it the same `variable_declarator` as any other initialiser, and a query cannot tell the value apart without reading its text, so the family is Value; the name and the span are still right."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "definitions"
      ],
      "reason": "Each head of an overload set is declared separately: a `function_signature` and the implementation that follows it are separate nodes under the same name, and nothing here says which implementation an overload belongs to. The anonymous members of an object type -- a call signature, a construct signature, an index signature -- are not declared at all, because there is no name to declare them under."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "imports",
        "modules"
      ],
      "reason": "A module path is stated exactly as written. Resolving './util' or '@app/core' to a file needs the tsconfig 'paths', 'baseUrl' and the extension rules of the target module system, none of which is in the file. A re-export names the module it re-exports from, but not the names that pass through it, and 'export *' introduces names that are nowhere in this source."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "calls",
        "references"
      ],
      "reason": "A call is stated under the name as written. 'svc.save()' is a call to 'save' with the receiver unknown, because a TypeScript type is erased before anything runs and this Pack resolves no types; a call through a variable that holds a function, or through 'apply'/'Reflect', is not stated at all. A property that is read rather than called is not emitted, because the grammar gives a read and a callee the same 'member_expression' and a bare member name would resolve against every declaration of that name in the repository."
    },
    {
      "query": "queries.scm",
      "capabilities": [
        "types"
      ],
      "reason": "A type is stated as a mention of each 'type_identifier' written in it, at every depth. The built-in types ('string', 'number', 'void') are 'predefined_type' and are not emitted, because they resolve to no declaration. A type parameter's own name is a mention rather than a declaration, so 'T' in one file does not resolve to 'T' in another, and a type declaration's own name is also a mention of itself, because the grammar spells both as 'type_identifier'. Nothing is computed: a conditional, mapped, indexed-access, template-literal or 'infer' type is only the names written in it."
    },
